Name: listenTimeoutExpired

Type: message

Syntax: listenTimeoutExpired

Summary:
Sent to the current card of the default stack when the command window
that opened after a wake word detection expires without a registered
voice command being recognised.

Introduced: 1.0.0

OS: mac, Windows, Linux

Platforms: desktop

Example:
-- Notify the user that no command was heard in time
on listenTimeoutExpired
    play "timeout.aiff"
end listenTimeoutExpired

Example:
-- Hide the listening indicator when the window closes
on listenTimeoutExpired
    hide image "listeningIndicator"
end listenTimeoutExpired

Description:
When a wake word is configured with <setWakeWord>, the engine opens a
command window after the wake word is detected. If no registered phrase
is recognised before the window closes (after the number of milliseconds
specified in <setWakeWord>), <listenTimeoutExpired> is dispatched and
the engine returns to waiting for the wake word.

This message gives you an opportunity to inform the user that the engine
is no longer actively listening for a command.

<listenTimeoutExpired> is only dispatched when a wake word is set and
the command window times out. It is not sent when <stopListening> is
called directly.

References: setWakeWord (command), wakeWordDetected (message),
voiceCommand (message), startListening (command)

Tags: speech, voice
